    "qa.q1.title": "Is EN 1811 compliance the same as \"nickel-free\"?",
    "qa.q1.answer": "No. \"Nickel-free\" is an unregulated marketing phrase with no statutory definition under EU law. EN 1811 does not test whether an alloy contains zero nickel by weight. Instead, it measures the rate at which nickel ions leach out of the jewelry surface into artificial sweat over one week. An alloy containing nickel—such as implant-grade 316LVM stainless steel with 10% to 14% nickel—can meet the legal standard because its passive chromium oxide layer prevents nickel release. Conversely, an inferior alloy with only 1% nickel can fail if its surface releases freely.",
    "qa.q2.title": "What is the EU nickel limit under REACH Annex XVII, Entry 27?",
    "qa.q2.answer": "European REACH Regulation (EC) No 1907/2006, Annex XVII, Entry 27 specifies two strict release thresholds: less than 0.2 µg/cm²/week for post assemblies inserted into pierced ears and other pierced parts of the human body during epithelization and healed wear; and 0.5 µg/cm²/week for articles intended to come into direct and prolonged contact with the skin. For coated articles, the coating must ensure the release rate remains below the limit for at least two years of normal use, verified by EN 12472 mechanical wear simulation before EN 1811 testing.",
