    "std.limits.title": "The Legal Limits: REACH Annex XVII, Entry 27",
    "std.limits.intro": "Regulation (EC) No 1907/2006 (REACH) Annex XVII, Entry 27 (implementing EU Directive 94/27/EC, amended by 2004/96/EC) establishes statutory release thresholds across Europe:",
    "std.limit.piercing.title": "0.2 µg/cm²/week — Pierced Parts of the Body",
    "std.limit.piercing.desc": "Applies to any post assembly or article inserted into pierced ears and other pierced parts of the human body during epithelization of the wound caused by piercing, whether subsequently retained or not. This encompasses both initial piercing jewelry and healed piercing jewelry inserted into a tract.",
    "std.limit.contact.title": "0.5 µg/cm²/week — Direct & Prolonged Skin Contact",
    "std.limit.contact.desc": "Applies to articles intended to come into direct and prolonged contact with the skin, such as earrings, necklaces, bracelets, chains, anklets, finger rings, wristwatch cases and straps, rivets, buttons, tighteners, and zippers on garments.",
    "std.limit.coating.title": "Two-Year Durability for Coated Articles",
    "std.limit.coating.desc": "Coated non-nickel articles must ensure that the rate of nickel release does not exceed the relevant limit (0.2 or 0.5 µg/cm²/week) for a period of at least two years of normal use. Compliance is tested by subjecting the coated article to simulated accelerated wear and corrosion under EN 12472 before testing under EN 1811.",
    "std.what_is.title": "What EN 1811:2023 Is & How It Operates",
    "std.what_is.p1": "EN 1811:2023 is the official European standard method titled: \"Reference test method for release of nickel from all post assemblies which are inserted into pierced parts of the human body and articles intended to come into direct and prolonged contact with the skin\". It replaced EN 1811:2011+A1:2015.",
    "std.what_is.p2": "The test procedure immerses the test item in artificial sweat solution (containing sodium chloride, DL-lactic acid, and urea, pH adjusted with dilute ammonia) maintained at 30 ± 2 °C for exactly 168 ± 1 hours (one full week) without agitation. The quantity of dissolved nickel ions in the solution is then quantitatively measured using Inductively Coupled Plasma Optical Emission Spectrometry (ICP-OES) or Mass Spectrometry (ICP-MS), normalized against the exposed surface area in square centimeters.",
    "std.dmg.title": "The Dimethylglyoxime (DMG) Spot Test: Both Ways",
    "std.dmg.p1": "The DMG spot test (standardized in CEN/TR 12471) is a qualitative chemical screening method, not a substitute for EN 1811 testing.",
    "std.dmg.positive.title": "What a Positive Result Tells You",
    "std.dmg.positive.desc": "A strawberry-red complex indicates the presence of soluble nickel ions leaching readily from the surface at a concentration roughly above 0.5 µg/cm²/week. A positive test indicates the article is releasing nickel and is unsuitable for piercing wear.",
    "std.dmg.negative.title": "What a Negative Result DOES NOT Tell You",
    "std.dmg.negative.desc": "A negative result does NOT prove compliance with the 0.2 µg/cm²/week piercing limit. The chemical colorimetric threshold of DMG is less sensitive than laboratory ICP spectrometry. Furthermore, lacquer, thin gold flash, or passivation layers can temporarily mask underlying nickel that will release once worn.",
    "std.factors.title": "What Decides Whether Jewelry Releases Nickel",
    "std.factors.intro": "Release is not determined by multiplying composition by a surface number. It is governed by five metallurgical and biological factors:",
    "std.factor.alloy": "Crystal Lattice Bonding: How tightly nickel atoms are bound into the crystalline structure of the alloy.",
    "std.factor.passivation": "Passive Oxide Layer: Stable chromium oxide (Cr₂O₃) or titanium oxide (TiO₂) films isolate bulk metal from sweat.",
    "std.factor.finish": "Surface Finish: Micro-roughness and crevices increase true surface area and create micro-galvanic cells.",
    "std.factor.wear": "Coatings & Mechanical Wear: Plating rubs off over time; wear simulation (EN 12472) tests whether underlying layers expose nickel.",
    "std.factor.sweat": "Sweat Chemistry & pH: Individual skin pH (acidic sweat accelerates ion dissolution) and chloride ion concentrations.",
    "std.examples.title": "Which Limit Applies to What: Worked Examples",
    "std.examples.intro": "Statutory classification under REACH Annex XVII Entry 27:",
