    "std.changelog.title": "Regulatory Changelog: EN 1811 Revisions & Why Historical Reports Expire",
    "std.changelog.intro": "Understanding standard revisions is critical for piercers reviewing mill certificates and laboratory test reports. Test reports issued under superseded standards or without mandatory wear simulation do not guarantee legal compliance under REACH Annex XVII Entry 27 today.",
    "std.changelog.v1998.tag": "EN 1811:1998",
    "std.changelog.v1998.status": "Superseded",
    "std.changelog.v1998.summary": "Original European harmonized standard for Directive 94/27/EC. Assessed migration into artificial sweat over 1 week. Lacked standardized wear simulation for coated items and used a wide tolerance window.",
    "std.changelog.v2011.tag": "EN 1811:2011",
    "std.changelog.v2011.status": "Superseded",
    "std.changelog.v2011.summary": "Introduced an adjustment factor (0.1) that led to confusion, and established an \"inconclusive\" band (0.11 to 0.28 µg/cm²/week for piercing posts). Many suppliers misinterpreted inconclusive lab reports as passes.",
    "std.changelog.v2015.tag": "EN 1811:2011+A1:2015",
    "std.changelog.v2015.status": "Superseded",
    "std.changelog.v2015.summary": "Removed the 0.1 adjustment factor and clarified measurement uncertainty rules, but still retained an ambiguous inconclusive band. Often performed on un-aged plated items without wear pre-treatment.",
    "std.changelog.v2023.tag": "EN 1811:2023",
    "std.changelog.v2023.status": "Current European Standard",
    "std.changelog.v2023.summary": "Current official reference test method. Clarifies testing of piercing post assemblies, eliminates ambiguity in decision rules, tightens artificial sweat preparation tolerances, and strictly integrates with EN 12472:2020 wear pre-treatment for coated articles.",
    "std.changelog.outdated_title": "Why Historical Test Reports Might Be Legally Outdated",
    "std.changelog.reason1": "Measurement Uncertainty Decision Rules: EN 1811:2011 and A1:2015 permitted an \"inconclusive\" result band (0.11 to 0.28 µg/cm²/week for piercing posts) where laboratories did not declare a failure. Under modern enforcement and EN 1811:2023, strict decision rules prevent ambiguous passes from being marketed as compliant.",
    "std.changelog.reason2": "Mandatory Wear Simulation (EN 12472:2020): Older certificates on gold-plated, PVD, or rhodium-flashed body jewelry tested items in pristine, un-aged condition. REACH Annex XVII Entry 27 legally mandates a two-year simulated wear resistance; un-aged test reports are legally invalid for coated jewelry.",
    "std.changelog.reason3": "Universal Piercing Post Classification: Modern regulatory guidelines make clear that all post assemblies inserted into epithelialized or un-epithelialized piercing tracts fall under the 0.2 µg/cm²/week threshold. Older certificates attempting to classify healed piercing posts under the 0.5 µg/cm²/week skin contact limit are non-compliant.",
    "std.changelog.reason4": "Reagent Purity and Calibration Tightening: The 2023 update tightens sweat solution buffering and analytical ICP calibration tolerances, meaning marginal passes produced in older testing runs may fail modern accredited laboratory audits.",
    "coating.timeline.title": "Protective Coating Lifespan & EN 12472 Wear Simulation",
    "coating.timeline.subtitle": "EN 12472:2020 simulates 2 years of normal wear (abrasive tumbling in synthetic sweat) prior to EN 1811 testing. See why coatings degrade and why substrate selection is critical.",
    "coating.timeline.stage0": "0 Cycles (Factory Pristine)",
    "coating.timeline.stage1": "1,000–2,000 Cycles: Gold Flash Breach",
    "coating.timeline.stage2": "3,000–5,000 Cycles: Heavy Plating Edge Wear",
    "coating.timeline.stage3": "Simulated 2-Year EN 12472 Benchmark",
    "coating.timeline.stage4": "Extended Friction: PVD Ceramic Longevity",
    "coating.timeline.flash_desc": "Thin Gold Flash (<0.1 µm): Rapid micro-porosity and abrasion loss under synthetic sweat friction. If applied over leachable brass or nickel-silver, severe nickel release begins early.",
    "coating.timeline.plate_desc": "Heavy Micron Electroplate (1–2 µm): Higher durability but mechanical friction at threads, hinges, and seams eventually breaches the barrier. Must use a certified nickel-free barrier layer.",
    "coating.timeline.pvd_desc": "PVD on Biocompatible Core (0.5–2 µm TiN/ZrN): High-hardness ceramic molecular bond withstanding prolonged EN 12472 cycles. Critical rule: applied over ASTM F136 titanium, even complete coating wear exposes 0% nickel safe metal.",
    "std.search.label": "Search regulations and limits",
